Rule 72. Duties of Magistrate Judge; Referral of Cases to Magistrate Judge;
Objections to Report and Recommendations of Magistrate Judge.
(a) Duties.
(1) General Authorization. The full-time United States magistrate judge is authorized to
exercise all the powers and perform all duties conferred upon magistrate judges by 28
U.S.C. § 636, and to exercise the powers stated in the rules governing proceedings under
28 U.S.C. §§ 2254 and 2255.
(2) Other Duties. A magistrate judge is also authorized to:
(A) exercise general supervision of civil and criminal calendars and decide
motions to expedite or postpone trials;
(B) conduct pretrial conferences, settlement conferences, omnibus hearings, and
related pretrial proceedings in civil and criminal cases;
(C) conduct arraignments in criminal cases not triable by the magistrate judge and
take not guilty pleas in such cases;
(D) impanel grand juries and receive grand jury returns under Fed. R. Crim. P.
6(f);
(E) accept waivers of indictment under Fed. R. Crim. P. 7(b);
(F) conduct voir dire and select petit juries for the court;
